{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Keep the sabbath day to sanctify it.</b> In the first Decalogue (Exodus 20) it says זכור ('remember') and here שמור ('keep'). The reason is to obligate the women in Kiddush and Havdalah even though they are positive commandments bound to time, from which women are exempt. For the remembrance of the sanctity of Shabbat is juxtaposed to the guarding of it from labor, in which all are warned, both men and women; therefore they said: all who are included in guarding are included in remembering, and these women, since they are included in guarding, are included in remembering — both at its entry and at its departure. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: זכור and שמור were spoken in a single utterance." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: they were spoken in a single utterance, for He is the Master in whom is mercy. And understand that it is fitting that in the Torah He mention זכור, and in Mishneh Torah שמור, for thus you will find that in the Torah He always mentions the Unique Name, while in Mishneh Torah He always mentions Adonai, your Elohim / Adonai, your Elohim — as in the language of the Ten Commandments: אנכי ה' אלהיך ('I am YHWH your Elohim'), and He is the one who created heaven and earth. And so you will find in the creation of the world (Genesis 2): בהבראם בה' בראם ('when they were created, with ה He created them'). And the Torah was given in five voices, and likewise the world was created in six days, and the Torah was given on the sixth of Sivan. And in the work of Creation you will find אלהים thirty-two times, and the Torah begins with ב and ends with ל, and all this is to contemplate that He who created the world gave us the Torah, and understand this." "<b>As YHWH your Elohim commanded you.</b> At the standing at Mount Sinai, and that is what is written there (Exodus 20): וידבר אלהים ('and Elohim spoke')."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses 